1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,788
It is night, the hospital discharge counter.

2
00:00:02,788 --> 00:00:05,228
Papa's surgery went well,

3
00:00:05,228 --> 00:00:07,319
and everyone's face shows relief.

4
00:00:07,319 --> 00:00:09,759
Bhai is standing at the counter with the file.

5
00:00:09,759 --> 00:00:13,593
The billing man looks at the screen and says quietly,

6
00:00:13,593 --> 00:00:16,730
the insurance has cut a big part of the claim.

7
00:00:16,730 --> 00:00:19,403
Bhai doesn't understand. We had the policy,

8
00:00:19,403 --> 00:00:23,078
we paid the premium on time every year. So how did this get cut?

9
00:00:23,078 --> 00:00:25,083
Mummy's eyes are full of worry.

10
00:00:25,083 --> 00:00:28,090
His wife is checking the account balance on her phone.

11
00:00:28,090 --> 00:00:30,429
The money that was kept for the SIP,

12
00:00:30,429 --> 00:00:32,100
will now go to the hospital.

13
00:00:32,100 --> 00:00:34,857
This story is not only about this family.

14
00:00:34,857 --> 00:00:38,647
Many insured families get this same shock at the time of discharge.

15
00:00:38,647 --> 00:00:40,714
Most of the time the reason is not any fraud.

16
00:00:40,714 --> 00:00:43,126
The reason is the small print of the policy,

17
00:00:43,126 --> 00:00:45,193
which we never read.

18
00:00:45,193 --> 00:00:47,950
And that print is written perfectly clearly.

19
00:00:47,950 --> 00:00:52,440
So today let's read this small print line by line.

20
00:00:52,440 --> 00:00:53,130
First question,

21
00:00:53,130 --> 00:00:56,929
can the room rent limit cut the whole claim?

22
00:00:56,929 --> 00:01:00,728
Second question, what is not covered during the waiting period? And the last question,

23
00:01:00,728 --> 00:01:02,800
is the insurance from the office enough?

24
00:01:02,800 --> 00:01:05,301
Come, let's take the story back.

25
00:01:05,301 --> 00:01:07,445
Papa had pain in his chest,

26
00:01:07,445 --> 00:01:10,661
they took him to emergency at night. The doctor said,

27
00:01:10,661 --> 00:01:13,877
he will have to be admitted, there will be surgery. At the reception the question came,

28
00:01:13,877 --> 00:01:17,450
which room do you want? General ward, shared room, or private room?

29
00:01:17,450 --> 00:01:21,412
Bhai didn't think for even a second. He said, give a private room,

30
00:01:21,412 --> 00:01:25,015
Papa needs rest. The logic was simple. We have insurance, right?

31
00:01:25,015 --> 00:01:28,977
The company will pay the bill. Why be stingy about Papa's health?

32
00:01:28,977 --> 00:01:31,859
At that time this decision feels like love,

33
00:01:31,859 --> 00:01:33,300
not like a mistake at all.

34
00:01:33,300 --> 00:01:37,309
Now look at that page of the policy which nobody read.

35
00:01:37,309 --> 00:01:39,496
It said room rent limit.

36
00:01:39,496 --> 00:01:44,234
Room rent limit means the amount per day that the company will accept for the room rent,

37
00:01:44,234 --> 00:01:48,243
only that much. If you take a costlier room, you will pay the difference.

38
00:01:48,243 --> 00:01:50,430
Up to here it makes sense.

39
00:01:50,430 --> 00:01:54,041
The real shock is next, which is called proportionate deduction.

40
00:01:54,041 --> 00:01:56,850
Proportionate deduction means, the more times costlier the room is,

41
00:01:56,850 --> 00:02:00,862
The rest of the bill gets cut in the same proportion. Not just the rent,

42
00:02:00,862 --> 00:02:04,071
the doctor's fees, nursing, operation theatre charges,

43
00:02:04,071 --> 00:02:06,880
all of it gets cut in the same ratio.

44
00:02:06,880 --> 00:02:11,571
Because when the hospital charges more for the room, it raises the rate of every service too.

45
00:02:11,571 --> 00:02:14,587
Bigger room patient, bigger package. The company says,

46
00:02:14,587 --> 00:02:16,933
we promised you a smaller room.

47
00:02:16,933 --> 00:02:20,284
So we will pay for everything at the smaller room's rate.

48
00:02:20,284 --> 00:02:23,970
That is why one room decision ate up the whole bill.

49
00:02:23,970 --> 00:02:27,609
Then came the second shock, consumables. Consumables means gloves, masks, syringes,

50
00:02:27,609 --> 00:02:28,700
cotton and things like that,

51
00:02:28,700 --> 00:02:31,975
which are used once and thrown away.

52
00:02:31,975 --> 00:02:34,522
Many policies do not cover these at all.

53
00:02:34,522 --> 00:02:37,069
The bill has a long list of them,

54
00:02:37,069 --> 00:02:39,980
and every line comes out of your pocket.

55
00:02:39,980 --> 00:02:41,031
The third shock, sub-limit.

56
00:02:41,031 --> 00:02:46,284
Sub-limit means for some particular treatments the company has put a separate ceiling.

57
00:02:46,284 --> 00:02:49,086
Like cataract operation, or knee surgery.

58
00:02:49,086 --> 00:02:51,537
Your total cover may be big,

59
00:02:51,537 --> 00:02:55,390
but for that treatment you will get money only up to the ceiling.

60
00:02:55,390 --> 00:02:56,383
And the fourth, co-pay.

61
00:02:56,383 --> 00:03:00,025
Co-pay means in every claim you pay a fixed share yourself,

62
00:03:00,025 --> 00:03:01,680
no matter how big the bill is.

63
00:03:01,680 --> 00:03:04,990
In many policies this applies to senior citizens.

64
00:03:04,990 --> 00:03:08,301
The premium looks a little lower, so people are happy.

65
00:03:08,301 --> 00:03:11,280
But on the day of the bill, this share feels heavy.

66
00:03:11,280 --> 00:03:13,404
So the answer to the first question is, yes.

67
00:03:13,404 --> 00:03:16,944
A room rent limit can cut the whole claim.

68
00:03:16,944 --> 00:03:19,068
So ask at the time of admission itself,

69
00:03:19,068 --> 00:03:21,546
which room does my policy allow?

70
00:03:21,546 --> 00:03:24,732
Many policies have no room rent limit at all.

71
00:03:24,732 --> 00:03:27,210
Check your policy, don't guess.

72
00:03:27,210 --> 00:03:28,786
Now another house, another story.

73
00:03:28,786 --> 00:03:32,252
My sister bought a new health policy for Mummy last year.

74
00:03:32,252 --> 00:03:34,457
Mummy has sugar, and BP too.

75
00:03:34,457 --> 00:03:37,293
While filling the form the agent said, these are small things,

76
00:03:37,293 --> 00:03:40,760
no need to write them. My sister thought, the form will get filled quickly,

77
00:03:40,760 --> 00:03:42,020
and the premium will come lower too.

78
00:03:42,020 --> 00:03:46,120
A few months later Mummy had to go to the hospital. The claim was sent,

79
00:03:46,120 --> 00:03:49,810
and it came back with a letter. Claim rejected.

80
00:03:49,810 --> 00:03:54,320
The reason written was, waiting period for pre-existing disease, and hiding information.

81
00:03:54,320 --> 00:03:58,830
Behen was shocked. The policy was active, and the premium was paid too.

82
00:03:58,830 --> 00:04:01,838
First, understand the terms. Pre-existing disease means an illness

83
00:04:01,838 --> 00:04:05,975
that you already had before taking the policy. Like sugar, BP,

84
00:04:05,975 --> 00:04:08,607
thyroid, or some old heart problem.

85
00:04:08,607 --> 00:04:11,992
The company does not cover the cost of these illnesses from the start.

86
00:04:11,992 --> 00:04:15,000
For these, there is a waiting time.

87
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:17,601
This waiting time is called the waiting period.

88
00:04:17,601 --> 00:04:21,316
Waiting period means the time after taking the policy

89
00:04:21,316 --> 00:04:25,403
during which some claims are not paid. There is an initial waiting period,

90
00:04:25,403 --> 00:04:28,375
when you get almost nothing except for accidents.

91
00:04:28,375 --> 00:04:32,090
Then some specific illnesses have their own separate waiting period.

92
00:04:32,090 --> 00:04:35,461
And the longest is the waiting period for old illnesses.

93
00:04:35,461 --> 00:04:40,518
The good thing is that IRDAI has reduced the upper limit for this.

94
00:04:40,518 --> 00:04:44,226
IRDAI means the regulator of insurance companies, which makes their rules.

95
00:04:44,226 --> 00:04:47,260
Still, every policy has its own time period,

96
00:04:47,260 --> 00:04:49,620
which is written in its papers.

97
00:04:49,620 --> 00:04:51,160
Now look at Behen's mistake.

98
00:04:51,160 --> 00:04:53,933
Mummy's illness was hidden in the form.

99
00:04:53,933 --> 00:04:57,013
So even after the waiting period, the company could say,

100
00:04:57,013 --> 00:04:58,245
you did not tell the truth.

101
00:04:58,245 --> 00:05:01,018
Non-disclosure means not telling an important thing in the form.

102
00:05:01,018 --> 00:05:03,790
This is the most common reason for claim rejection.

103
00:05:03,790 --> 00:05:08,154
The agent's advice seems easy at that time. Low premium,

104
00:05:08,154 --> 00:05:12,082
short form, no medical test. But remember,

105
00:05:12,082 --> 00:05:16,446
at claim time, it's not the agent, it's the company that reads your file.

106
00:05:16,446 --> 00:05:19,938
And the company first opens the very form

107
00:05:19,938 --> 00:05:22,120
that you signed.

108
00:05:22,120 --> 00:05:25,142
Now a big rule, called moratorium.

109
00:05:25,142 --> 00:05:27,408
Moratorium means a wall of protection.

110
00:05:27,408 --> 00:05:30,052
After the policy runs continuously for 60 months,

111
00:05:30,052 --> 00:05:34,206
the company cannot question a claim on the grounds of non-disclosure.

112
00:05:34,206 --> 00:05:36,850
Only a proven fraud case is different.

113
00:05:36,850 --> 00:05:39,881
One word in this rule is very important: continuously.

114
00:05:39,881 --> 00:05:42,913
If the policy lapsed in between, and was not renewed,

115
00:05:42,913 --> 00:05:45,271
then the count can start again from the beginning.

116
00:05:45,271 --> 00:05:48,976
So never miss the renewal date. Set up auto debit,

117
00:05:48,976 --> 00:05:50,660
or keep a reminder in your calendar.

118
00:05:50,660 --> 00:05:52,592
Now, the answer to the second question.

119
00:05:52,592 --> 00:05:57,614
In the waiting period, you don't get claims for old illness and some specific treatments.

120
00:05:57,614 --> 00:05:58,773
And if you hid the truth,

121
00:05:58,773 --> 00:06:02,250
So even after the waiting period, a claim can get stuck.

122
00:06:02,250 --> 00:06:04,568
That's why write down every illness in the form.

123
00:06:04,568 --> 00:06:08,045
Even the 60-month wall only helps when

124
00:06:08,045 --> 00:06:09,590
the policy keeps running without a break.

125
00:06:09,590 --> 00:06:12,836
Third story, about an office worker. Bhai has a good job,

126
00:06:12,836 --> 00:06:15,108
and the company has given him group health insurance.

127
00:06:15,108 --> 00:06:20,301
Group insurance means one policy taken by the company for all its employees together.

128
00:06:20,301 --> 00:06:23,222
Bhai says, why do I need a separate policy,

129
00:06:23,222 --> 00:06:24,520
I have the office one, don't I?

130
00:06:24,520 --> 00:06:27,162
His logic doesn't seem wrong. The company pays the premium,

131
00:06:27,162 --> 00:06:28,923
you get cover from day one.

132
00:06:28,923 --> 00:06:31,565
Many group policies have no waiting period either.

133
00:06:31,565 --> 00:06:34,207
Sometimes parents are covered along with you too.

134
00:06:34,207 --> 00:06:36,262
Everything is coming without doing anything,

135
00:06:36,262 --> 00:06:37,730
so why spend our own money?

136
00:06:37,730 --> 00:06:40,676
Then one day an email came from the office: restructuring.

137
00:06:40,676 --> 00:06:43,622
Bhai lost his job. The salary stopped,

138
00:06:43,622 --> 00:06:45,831
and the health insurance went with it.

139
00:06:45,831 --> 00:06:49,513
A group policy is tied to the job. When the job goes,

140
00:06:49,513 --> 00:06:52,091
the cover ends right along with it.

141
00:06:52,091 --> 00:06:54,300
Often you don't even get a long notice.

142
00:06:54,300 --> 00:06:57,636
Now think, in that same month Papa has to go to the hospital again.

143
00:06:57,636 --> 00:06:59,971
No office cover, no policy of your own.

144
00:06:59,971 --> 00:07:01,972
And now if you go to buy a new policy,

145
00:07:01,972 --> 00:07:05,975
the premium will be very high because of Papa's age and illness.

146
00:07:05,975 --> 00:07:08,310
On top of that, the waiting period starts all over again.

147
00:07:08,310 --> 00:07:10,532
The second problem: the cover is shared by everyone.

148
00:07:10,532 --> 00:07:14,976
In an office policy, often one cover is for the whole family.

149
00:07:14,976 --> 00:07:16,881
Papa had a big operation,

150
00:07:16,881 --> 00:07:20,055
so the year's cover ends right there. Then the daughter falls ill,

151
00:07:20,055 --> 00:07:21,960
and there is nothing left for her.

152
00:07:21,960 --> 00:07:25,662
And the third problem, retirement. The day you retire,

153
00:07:25,662 --> 00:07:28,624
the office insurance retires too.

154
00:07:28,624 --> 00:07:32,697
And the risk of illness is highest at exactly that age.

155
00:07:32,697 --> 00:07:36,770
At that time, buying a new policy is both expensive and difficult.

156
00:07:36,770 --> 00:07:39,118
So the answer to the third question is: no.

157
00:07:39,118 --> 00:07:43,423
Office insurance is a nice bonus, but it's not enough on its own.

158
00:07:43,423 --> 00:07:45,771
You should have your own separate policy,

159
00:07:45,771 --> 00:07:50,075
one that stays with you even when you change jobs. And the sooner you take it,

160
00:07:50,075 --> 00:07:54,380
the sooner the waiting period and the 60-month count will start.

161
00:07:54,380 --> 00:07:56,788
Now, the biggest mistake.

162
00:07:56,788 --> 00:08:00,800
When we buy a policy, we only look at the premium and the cover.

163
00:08:00,800 --> 00:08:02,806
How much we have to pay, how much we get.

164
00:08:02,806 --> 00:08:05,615
But claims get cut because of the things

165
00:08:05,615 --> 00:08:09,627
written in the middle. Room rent, sub-limit, co-pay,

166
00:08:09,627 --> 00:08:10,430
waiting period.

167
00:08:10,430 --> 00:08:12,748
First thing: read the Customer Information Sheet.

168
00:08:12,748 --> 00:08:15,838
The Customer Information Sheet is a short summary of the policy,

169
00:08:15,838 --> 00:08:19,314
with all the important terms written in one place.

170
00:08:19,314 --> 00:08:23,563
Every company has to give it. Check the room rent, co-pay,

171
00:08:23,563 --> 00:08:27,040
sub-limit and every waiting period in it. Read it with a pen in hand.

172
00:08:27,040 --> 00:08:30,011
Second thing: understand the difference between cashless and reimbursement.

173
00:08:30,011 --> 00:08:34,468
Cashless means the bill is settled directly between the company and the hospital.

174
00:08:34,468 --> 00:08:38,553
This happens at a network hospital. A network hospital is a hospital

175
00:08:38,553 --> 00:08:40,782
that has a tie-up with the company.

176
00:08:40,782 --> 00:08:43,010
There you have to take approval first.

177
00:08:43,010 --> 00:08:46,461
Reimbursement means you first pay the bill from your own pocket,

178
00:08:46,461 --> 00:08:50,680
then submit the papers and ask the company for the money back. For this, you have to keep the original bills,

179
00:08:50,680 --> 00:08:54,131
reports and discharge summary safe.

180
00:08:54,131 --> 00:08:57,583
If one paper is lost, the claim can get stuck.

181
00:08:57,583 --> 00:08:59,500
So make a separate file.

182
00:08:59,500 --> 00:09:03,837
The good news is that IRDAI has tightened the time limit for cashless discharge.

183
00:09:03,837 --> 00:09:06,728
The company has to give discharge approval within hours,

184
00:09:06,728 --> 00:09:08,752
not make you wait all day. If there is a delay,

185
00:09:08,752 --> 00:09:10,775
ask the insurance desk in writing.

186
00:09:10,775 --> 00:09:12,510
There is also a way to file a complaint.

187
00:09:12,510 --> 00:09:16,678
Third thing: write the truth in the form. Every illness, every medicine,

188
00:09:16,678 --> 00:09:20,013
every past surgery. The premium may go up a little,

189
00:09:20,013 --> 00:09:24,598
but on the day of the claim your file will be clean. And the fourth thing:

190
00:09:24,598 --> 00:09:27,933
keep your own policy apart from the office policy,

191
00:09:27,933 --> 00:09:29,600
especially for your parents.

192
00:09:29,600 --> 00:09:30,812
There is one more benefit.

193
00:09:30,812 --> 00:09:34,450
Under the old tax regime, you get a tax deduction on health insurance premium,

194
00:09:34,450 --> 00:09:37,480
for yourself and your parents. So the premium is not just an expense,

195
00:09:37,480 --> 00:09:38,996
it saves a bit of tax too.

196
00:09:38,996 --> 00:09:42,330
Do check your own calculation with your CA or tax advisor.

197
00:09:42,330 --> 00:09:43,299
And remember,

198
00:09:43,299 --> 00:09:48,143
the real job of health insurance is to protect your SIP and savings on the day of illness.

199
00:09:48,143 --> 00:09:50,727
If the claim gets cut, people take loans,

200
00:09:50,727 --> 00:09:53,310
or stop their SIP. Have the right policy,

201
00:09:53,310 --> 00:09:56,540
So you don't have to do this. This is not investment advice.

202
00:09:56,540 --> 00:09:58,606
Now, one straight question for you.

203
00:09:58,606 --> 00:10:02,739
Does your health policy have a room rent limit or not?

204
00:10:02,739 --> 00:10:06,458
Don't answer without looking. Open your policy, check it,

205
00:10:06,458 --> 00:10:10,590
and in the comments just write: yes, no, or don't know.

206
00:10:10,590 --> 00:10:11,830
I will read everything.

207
00:10:11,830 --> 00:10:16,146
In the next video, we'll talk about term insurance. How much cover you need,

208
00:10:16,146 --> 00:10:19,285
and why people end up buying the wrong policy.

209
00:10:19,285 --> 00:10:22,425
Until then, send this video to the person

210
00:10:22,425 --> 00:10:25,171
who only has the insurance from their office.

211
00:10:25,171 --> 00:10:27,133
See you in the next video.
